sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Exp+Hematol%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2010 Jul;38(7):548-56.\n\ndoi: 10.1016/j.exphem.2010.03.004. Epub 2010 Mar 18.\n\n# Fibrocytes in health and disease\n\n[Erica L Herzog](/?term=Herzog+EL&cauthor_id=20303382)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Internal Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Yale University School of Medicine, New Haven, CT 06520-8057, USA. erica.herzog@yale.edu\") , [Richard Bucala](/?term=Bucala+R&cauthor_id=20303382)\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 Department of Internal Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Yale University School of Medicine, New Haven, CT 06520-8057, USA. erica.herzog@yale.edu\n\n-   PMID: **20303382**\n-   PMCID: [PMC3136351](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3136351/)\n-   DOI: [10.1016/j.exphem.2010.03.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.exphem.2010.03.004)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nReview\n\n# Fibrocytes in health and disease\n\nErica L Herzog et al. Exp Hematol. 2010 Jul.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nExp Hematol\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Exp+Hematol%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Exp+Hematol%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2010 Jul;38(7):548-56.\n\ndoi: 10.1016/j.exphem.2010.03.004. Epub 2010 Mar 18.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Erica L Herzog](/?term=Herzog+EL&cauthor_id=20303382)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Internal Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Yale University School of Medicine, New Haven, CT 06520-8057, USA. erica.herzog@yale.edu\") , [Richard Bucala](/?term=Bucala+R&cauthor_id=20303382)\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 Department of Internal Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Yale University School of Medicine, New Haven, CT 06520-8057, USA. erica.herzog@yale.edu\n\n-   PMID: **20303382**\n-   PMCID: [PMC3136351](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3136351/)\n-   DOI: [10.1016/j.exphem.2010.03.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.exphem.2010.03.004)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nFibrocytes are circulating mesenchymal progenitor cells that participate in tissue responses to injury and invasion. Accumulating knowledge from animal models regarding the differentiation, trafficking, and function of these cells implicates them in the development of diseases characterized by chronic inflammation and excessive collagen deposition. Recent data obtained from the clinical setting suggests that the enumeration of circulating fibrocytes may be a biomarker for disease progression in chronic lung diseases including asthma and pulmonary fibrosis. A greater understanding of the immunologic mediators that influence fibrocyte biology suggests new opportunities for therapeutic manipulation of these cells in fibrogenesis. This review integrates new developments in the cellular and molecular biology of fibrocytes with current concepts regarding the etiopathogenesis of fibrosing disorders.\n\nCopyright 2010 ISEH - Society for Hematology and Stem Cells. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nConflict of interest disclosure:\n\nDr. Bucala is on the Scientific Advisory Board of Promedior, Inc., which is developing agents to inhibit fibrosis.\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c0ce/3136351/75077e236b6e/nihms208539f1.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c0ce/3136351/c8d5cd094103/nihms208539f1.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nElectron micrograph of a fibrocyte…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nElectron micrograph of a fibrocyte in the dermis of a patient with nephrogenic…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nElectron micrograph of a fibrocyte in the dermis of a patient with nephrogenic systemic fibrosis (image provided by Shawn Cowper, MD).\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c0ce/3136351/176cd5c7c7f9/nihms208539f2.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c0ce/3136351/5229139067a3/nihms208539f2.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nFibrocyte origin, differentiation, migration and…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nFibrocyte origin, differentiation, migration and function.\n\n**Figure 2**\n\nFibrocyte origin, differentiation, migration and function.\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Wilson MS, Wynn TA. Pulmonary fibrosis: pathogenesis, etiology and regulation. Mucosal Immunol. 2009;2:103–121. - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2675823/) - [PubMed](/19129758/)\n2.  1.  Lee CG, Homer RJ, Cohn L, et al. Transgenic overexpression of interleukin (IL)-10 in the lung causes mucus metaplasia, tissue inflammation, and airway remodeling via IL-13-dependent and -independent pathways. J Biol Chem. 2002;277:35466–35474. - [PubMed](/12107190/)\n3.  1.  Noble PW, Homer RJ. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis: new insights into pathogenesis. Clin Chest Med. 2004;25:749–758. vii. - [PubMed](/15564020/)\n4.  1.  Liu Y. Renal fibrosis: new insights into the pathogenesis and therapeutics. Kidney Int. 2006;69:213–217. - [PubMed](/16408108/)\n5.  1.  Falk E. Pathogenesis of atherosclerosis. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2006;47:C7–C12. - [PubMed](/16631513/)\n\nShow all 69 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Research+Support%2C+N.I.H.%2C+Extramural%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Research+Support%2C+N.I.H.%2C+Extramural)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n    \n    Actions\n    \n  